## Weight-format comparison (all W4A16, f16 acts, apples-to-apples)
nvfp4 standard scales : 0.05003   (slightly worse than q4_0)
q4_0                  : 0.04815
nvfp4 no-subnormal    : 0.04028   <- BEATS q4_0 by -16%

CONCLUSION:
- subnormal fix flips nvfp4 weight format from behind q4_0 (0.0500) to ahead (0.0403 vs 0.0482).
  Free, global exponent shift, no per-tensor storage, no new type.
- DEPLOYED native FP4 (W4A4) stays behind q4_0 native (W4A8): activation-limited (+0.029 from fp4
  acts). Native FP4 requires W4A4 (OMMA is fp4xfp4), so the activation penalty is intrinsic to the
  speed path. q4_0 is ~insensitive to A8-vs-A16 (int8 acts near-lossless).

## Native FP4 epilogue attempt (lifted gguf on the OMMA) - BROKEN
[SUPERSEDED 2026-05-22 - the "kernel bug" diagnosis below is REFUTED. The breakage was the
 write-back hack's path-coverage gap, not the OMMA. See "Native kernel WORKS" at the end.]
Tried lifting scales + write-back compensation (mmq.cuh:3257, *2^-6 gated NVFP4):
  lifted, dequant W4A16        : KLD 0.0403, top-p 92.49%  (correct, same hardware __nv_fp8_e4m3 decode)
  lifted, native W4A4, no epi  : KLD 12.64,  top-p 0.000%  (catastrophic, non-uniform)
  lifted, native W4A4, *2^-6   : KLD 3.87,   top-p 26.05%  (still broken, non-uniform)
  N4_0  , native W4A4, *2^-6   : KLD 11.77,  top-p 0.05%   (expected - unlifted corrupted by /64)

=> native FP4 OMMA rejects rebiased (normal-range) weight scales NON-uniformly. Uniform 2^-N can't
fix (global factor preserves argmax; top-p ->0 means per-block corruption). Same lifted gguf is fine
on dequant. LIKELY A GGML KERNEL BUG: real NVFP4 runs block scales up to 448 on this OMMA; ggml's
native NVFP4 path only ever saw subnormal scales (99.94%), normal-range untested. Suspect
load_tiles_nvfp4 scale packing / scale_vec::4X setup (mma.cuh:1145).

FORK: weight-quality fix works only W4A16 (0.0403, beats q4_0, no FP4 speed) XOR native FP4 speed
(stuck ~0.0791, W4A4-activation-limited, behind q4_0). Unlocking N4_K on the speed path needs the
native kernel's normal-range scale handling fixed first.

## Native-kernel bug LOCALIZED (2026-05-22) - warp-cooperative A-scale operand, masked by uniformity
[SUPERSEDED / REFUTED 2026-05-22 - this localization was WRONG. The native OMMA handles
 normal-range high-variance scales correctly; proof in "Native kernel WORKS" at the end. The
 26% was the *write-back* compensation missing matmul paths (MMVQ/stream-k/router) + compounding
 across layers, exactly as PR #22196/#21074 review predicted (lanes select rows, not bytes).]
Re-ran lifted gguf on stock-codec build (common.cuh NOT patched) + epilogue only: top-p 26.05%,
PPL ratio 40.8 - IDENTICAL to the old run. So the codec patch was never the cause; the activation
quantizer (quantize.cu:132) was a red herring.

Instrumented the native path (host + device printf, --chunks 1) for absolute clarity:
  [N4_K-DISPATCH] use_native_fp4=1 ids=0 (blk.0.attn_qkv) -> native OMMA path confirmed.
  [N4_K-LT] load_tiles_nvfp4_nvfp4 runs; scale bytes load correctly and VARY within each 4-block
            group (e.g. "29 40 25 28", "30 48 42 41"; exp 3-6, all normal range).
  [N4_K-WB] write-back epilogue fires, exact /64 (0.013245->0.000207, 0.014732->0.000230).
=> epilogue path is used and uniform /64 is applied. 26% is NOT a skipped/misapplied epilogue.
   no-epi top-p 0% was mostly compounding 64x overflow across 65 layers, not per-block corruption.

ROOT CAUSE (localized): the scale_vec::4X MMA (mma.cuh:1145) consumes the four per-16 UE4M3 weight
scales as a WARP-COOPERATIVE packed register (vec_dot_fp4_fp4_mma:1020 "2 threads per quad supply
the packed scale register", PTX {0,0} thread/byte selectors; lane map tidx_A = tx/4 + (tx%2)*8 at
mmq.cuh:1022). That lane->sub-block mapping is only correct-by-coincidence when the 4 scales are
near-equal. test-backend-ops PASSES because uniform[-1,1] init -> per-16 amax ~0.94 -> scales ~0.157
with TINY variance -> a lane permutation swaps near-equal values -> error under NMSE tol. The
all-subnormal deployed format (99.94% subnormal ~ equal tiny scales) hid it the same way. Lifted
gguf has HIGH per-block amax variance -> the 4 scales differ a lot -> permutation swaps very
different values -> per-block corruption -> top-p collapse. Dequant is immune (decodes each block's
scale independently, no warp cooperation). Only the WEIGHT (A) scale changed between standard (89%,
works) and lifted (26%, broken); both share the same B/activation path -> bug is in the A-scale
operand assembly.

NEXT: deterministic high-variance test (per-16 scales deliberately spread, e.g. 0.5 vs 0.01 in
adjacent sub-blocks) comparing native MMQ vs CPU dequant -> will fail and pin the exact permutation;
candidate fix sites: tidx_A lane map, x_sc column index (k0/tile_A::J), or the {0,0} PTX selectors.

## Native kernel WORKS - subnormal rescue runs on the FP4 OMMA via the .scale epilogue (2026-05-22)

The "kernel bug" above is refuted. PR review (#22196/#21074) showed the native scale operand is
magnitude-independent (the four UE4M3 scales travel in one register, scale_vec::4X selectors {0,0};
tidx_A picks the row, not the byte) and the generic path decodes each scale independently. The clean
test confirms it: do the magnitude compensation in the GRAPH (per-tensor .scale via build_lora_mm's
ggml_mul), not in the MMQ write-back. Then it covers EVERY matmul uniformly, kernel-agnostic.

Mechanism (all on a STOCK inference binary, no write-back hack):
- Quantizer (ggml-quants.c quantize_row_nvfp4_ref/_impl): encode the scale lifted into the normal
  UE4M3 grid (arg *= 64) but compute E2M1 codes against the true-magnitude scale (decode /= 64).
  Global ue4m3 codec stays stock, so the stored byte reads 64x too large on every path.
- Producer (#22897 port in llama-quant.cpp): per-tensor .scale = sum(f32*dq)/sum(dq^2). Because the
  stock decode reads the lifted byte at 64x, the LS formula AUTO-derives .scale = 2^-6 * bias
  (measured ~0.01564, bias ~1.001) - no hand-coded 2^-6 needed.
- Consumer: build_lora_mm / build_ffn apply .scale as ggml_mul after the matmul.

Coverage caveat (the load-failure debugging): the .scale epilogue only covers matmul weights. Two
NVFP4 paths have no .scale application and MUST stay unlifted (quantized q8_0):
  - token_embd (read via get_rows) -> 64x embeddings from layer 0.
  - nextn.eh_proj (build_lora_mm with no _s arg) + #22897 over-emits its scale -> done_getting_tensors
    "expected 1874, got 1872" load failure (2 unconsumed scale tensors).
Built with --token-embedding-type q8_0 --tensor-type eh_proj=q8_0 -> loads, runs, coherent.

Result (qwen3.6-27b-nvfp4-canon2.gguf, 200 chunks, native FP4 path, vs the same f16 base):

| config                              | acts        | weight scales | Mean KLD            | top-p   |
|-------------------------------------|-------------|---------------|---------------------|---------|
| nvfp4 native, OLD (subnormal)       | fp4  (W4A4) | subnormal     | 0.07907 +/- 0.00175 | 89.041% |
| nvfp4 native, NEW (lift + .scale)   | fp4  (W4A4) | normal        | 0.06880 +/- 0.00155 | 89.737% |
| nvfp4 dequant, canon2 (W4A16)       | f16  (W4A16)| normal        | 0.03961 +/- 0.00134 | 92.461% |
| q4_0 native                         | int8 (W4A8) | fp16          | 0.04852 +/- 0.00149 | 91.737% |

(W4A16 control: GGML_NVFP4_FORCE_DEQUANT=1, env gate in ggml_cuda_should_use_mmq -> cuBLAS dequant.)

- Subnormal rescue NOW lands on the native speed path: 0.07907 -> 0.06880, -13% KLD, free (no new
  type; normal-grid placement + .scale epilogue). The doc's "speed XOR quality" fork is half-closed:
  the weight rescue is no longer dequant-only.
- As a WEIGHT format (W4A16), canon2 nvfp4 0.03961 BEATS q4_0 0.04852 by -18% (top-p 92.46 vs
  91.74), and edges the old global-pow2 ceiling (0.04028) - the per-tensor LS .scale is a touch
  better than a uniform 2^-6 shift.
- Still activation-limited on the speed path: 0.06880 (native W4A4) - 0.03961 (canon2 W4A16) =
  0.02919 = the fp4 activation penalty (matches the prior +0.029). So native nvfp4 stays ~42% behind
  q4_0 native (0.0688 vs 0.0485); the weight fix narrows but cannot close it (W4A4 is intrinsic to
  the OMMA - fp4 x fp4, no int8-activation option).
